{
  "page": 70,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "338",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        39.52751922607422,
        57.665863037109375,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        78.57550048828125,
        39.52751922607422,
        316.3913879394531,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 3.1 because we addressed a much more difﬁcult scenario where T = 500, σ = 1 and σx = √10,",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        50.321937561035156,
        438.2222595214844,
        66.93677520751953
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i.e. the data set was ﬁve times larger and we used the bootstrap ﬁlter in a very unfavourable scenario",
      "bbox": [
        42.70892333984375,
        67.34110260009766,
        438.23291015625,
        75.6529541015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where the likelihood of the observations is peaked and the noise of the dynamic diffuse. This is in",
      "bbox": [
        42.70892333984375,
        77.3036880493164,
        438.2204284667969,
        85.61553955078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "contrast with the scenario that is considered by Johannes, Polson and Yae where σ = √10 and σx = 1,",
      "bbox": [
        42.70892333984375,
        80.2096939086914,
        438.2220153808594,
        96.82465362548828
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i.e. the likelihood is fairly diffuse and the bootstrap ﬁlter and conditional bootstrap ﬁlters can provide",
      "bbox": [
        42.708709716796875,
        97.2291030883789,
        438.23016357421875,
        105.54095458984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "good proposals for a number of particles as small as 150, which is in agreement with Fig. 3 of the paper.",
      "bbox": [
        42.708709716796875,
        107.19168853759766,
        438.2255554199219,
        115.5035400390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover our PG sampler samples only .N −1/T random variables Xn and one set of parameters",
      "bbox": [
        42.708709716796875,
        116.4011001586914,
        438.2238464355469,
        126.81121826171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": ".σ, β1, β2, β3, σx/ per MCMC iteration whereas the particle ﬁlter using Gibbs moves needs to sample",
      "bbox": [
        42.70880126953125,
        126.3638687133789,
        438.22381591796875,
        136.1432342529297
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "NT random variables .Xn, σ, β1, β2, β3, σx/. As a result, for the computational complexity of using the",
      "bbox": [
        42.709747314453125,
        136.32644653320312,
        438.2243347167969,
        146.1058807373047
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "bootstrap ﬁlter with Gibbs moves for N =300000, we can run the PG sampler for 12000 iterations using a",
      "bbox": [
        42.70904541015625,
        146.28903198242188,
        438.2242431640625,
        155.35406494140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "conditional SMC sampler using 150 particles, which is more than sufﬁcient in this context. The MATLAB",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        157.0048065185547,
        438.22052001953125,
        165.316650390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "program runs in 7 min on a desktop computer. Fig. 22 displays the results. We ran many realizations",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        166.96739196777344,
        438.2188415527344,
        175.27923583984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "initialized with this very informative prior and the algorithm consistently returned virtually identical",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        176.9299774169922,
        438.2214050292969,
        185.2418212890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "results. Using vague priors for all parameters, we observed that poorly initialized PG samplers can some-",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        186.8925323486328,
        438.2249450683594,
        195.20437622070312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "times become trapped in some modes (and we conjecture that this might be so even for the ‘exact’ Gibbs",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        196.85508728027344,
        438.2213134765625,
        205.16693115234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sampler) but also manages to escape, in which case the results are very similar, and stable.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        206.81764221191406,
        381.6827697753906,
        215.12948608398438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For the same data set and the same informative prior, we ran 10 runs of the bootstrap ﬁlter with Gibbs",
      "bbox": [
        51.674530029296875,
        216.7801971435547,
        438.2186584472656,
        225.092041015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "steps for N =100000 particles. For some parameters, the results were quite similar among runs. However,",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        225.98956298828125,
        438.22406005859375,
        235.05459594726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "we also observed signiﬁcant variability in the estimates as illustrated in Fig. 23. As expected this variance",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        236.70530700683594,
        438.22857666015625,
        245.01715087890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "increases with time as a result of the path degeneracy phenomenon. Using vague priors for all parameters,",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        246.66786193847656,
        438.22320556640625,
        254.97970581054688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the procedure appeared unable to produce sensible approximations of the posterior.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7081298828125,
        256.6304016113281,
        358.9530029296875,
        264.9422607421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We conjecture that the variance of the approximation error of p.θ, x1:T |y1:T / increases superlinearly with",
      "bbox": [
        51.674530029296875,
        265.83978271484375,
        438.2229309082031,
        276.1523742675781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "T for such algorithms.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7088623046875,
        276.55670166015625,
        127.62068176269531,
        284.8685607910156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
